  "pd": {
    "basis": "traditional",
    "detail": "Traditional outward-bound capstan shanty sung on the nineteenth-century Australian wool run, in oral circulation among merchant crews well before 1900. No identifiable author.",
    "sourceEdition": "Traditional oral repertoire of the nineteenth-century Australian wool run; no single published score was read, and none is claimed. What is carried is the bare unaccompanied melody line plus plain I-IV-V bar changes, which is the shanty itself",
    "notes": "This song ships with NO WORDS, deliberately. The earliest printing of the text is Laura Smith, 'The Music of the Waters', 1888, and it reads 'South Australia is my native home / Heave away! Heave away!', names Liza Lee and never mentions Cape Horn -- it is nothing like the version everybody sings. The familiar text ('In South Australia I was born', 'you rolling king', 'hear me sing', the Nancy Blair verse) is a mid-twentieth-century folk-revival assembly: 'hear me sing' is peculiar to Doerflinger's 1951 collection and reached the repertoire through A. L. Lloyd's 1957-58 recordings. That wording cannot be given a pre-1900 anonymous basis, so under the SPEC's lyrics policy it is not carried, and the Smith text is not reconstructed from a summary either. The tune stays. This block covers the music only. This tune reached most modern listeners through mid-twentieth-century revival recordings whose ARRANGEMENTS remain in copyright. Only the traditional melodic skeleton and plain I-IV-V bar changes are encoded here; none of those arrangements' added material, accompaniment or harmonisation is reproduced, and an unadorned folk melody acquires no new copyright by being popularised. Confidence is deliberately medium: the phrase shape of the call, the two-fold crew answer and the closing descent are certain, the interior passing notes are reconstructed. The tune is duple and sits in 4/4 with straight eighths; there are no triplet or compound-meter figures. The call begins with a single pickup note, which is carried at the END of the preceding section per the SPEC pickup rule, so no event uses a negative beat; every section therefore hands the next one its pickup."
  },
